    <node id="Tax Payable">
      <data key="d0">Tax Payable</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tax payable refers to the amount of tax that an individual or entity owes to the government after applying deductions and credits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-041dc2e0b32226e0b7c758a7359523f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101193</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Maximum Contribution for the Year">
      <data key="d0">Maximum Contribution for the Year</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The maximum contribution refers to the highest allowable amount for CPP contributions within a given year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-041dc2e0b32226e0b7c758a7359523f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101193</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Maximum Premium for the Year">
      <data key="d0">Maximum Premium for the Year</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The maximum premium refers to the upper limit for EI contributions that can be paid in a given year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-041dc2e0b32226e0b7c758a7359523f3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101193</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T1 Return">
      <data key="d0">T1 Return</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7c5cf2323ead79ef1bb328c9701238d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d66ea5179b3a914a2b6215ce0d8004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d33a716738be034afb001ad0bfbefeb</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple T4 Slips are combined on the T1 return to report total income for tax purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;The T1 return is a tax form used in Canada for reporting personal income tax, including capital gains.&lt;SEP&gt;The T1 Return is an income tax return form filed by individuals in Canada, used to assess eligibility for various credits and rebates such as the GST/HST Credit.&lt;SEP&gt;The T1 Return is the federal income tax return form that residents of Canada, including Quebec, must file annually.</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772370221</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CRA T1 General Income Tax and Benefit Return">
      <data key="d0">CRA T1 General Income Tax and Benefit Return</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">CRA T1 General Income Tax and Benefit Return is a tax form used for filing federal income tax returns in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101247</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Income Tax Act">
      <data key="d0">Income Tax Act</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">The Income Tax Act is the legislative framework governing income tax in Canada, including tax brackets and credits.&lt;SEP&gt;The Income Tax Act is the legislation that outlines tax obligations and regulations for individuals and corporations in Canada, including rules for RRSPs.&lt;SEP&gt;The Income Tax Act is a set of laws governing taxation in Canada, providing the framework for tax planning strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Income Tax Act is a Canadian federal law that governs income taxation and is referenced for federal benefits and credits.&lt;SEP&gt;The Income Tax Act is legislation that governs taxation laws in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d66ea5179b3a914a2b6215ce0d8004&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da06f6e3dca271d80baa141c0aef4d6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Federal Income Tax">
      <data key="d0">Federal Income Tax</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Federal Income Tax refers to taxes levied by the federal government on individual earnings, calculated through a progressive bracket system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101247</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basic Personal Amount">
      <data key="d0">Basic Personal Amount</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Basic Personal Amount is a non-refundable tax credit that reduces federal tax owed by individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;The Basic Personal Amount is the amount of income that can be earned tax-free and has been increased to $15,705 for the 2024 tax year.&lt;SEP&gt;The Basic Personal Amount is a non-refundable tax credit that reduces the amount of income tax owed by individuals in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The Basic Personal Amount is a non-refundable tax credit that reduces personal income tax, specified for New Brunswick over several tax years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03bb900cab8fd043392cad33c9c60640&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11a41d513daa1b6c57ef311273744d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe8637be3ac5b554a9bb36bec631101a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369992</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2024 Tax Year">
      <data key="d0">2024 Tax Year</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2024 Tax Year is a period during which tax rates and brackets apply for calculating federal income tax in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2024 tax year is the period for which certain tax deadlines and events are relevant, as mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2024 Tax Year encompasses anticipated changes to tax rates and regulations detailed in associated budgets.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2024 Tax Year represents the fiscal policies and tax brackets established for that specific year within Alberta.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2024 Tax Year includes specific income brackets and tax rates for assessing taxable income.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2024 Tax Year includes significant changes to tax brackets and the Basic Personal Amount in Manitoba.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2024 Tax Year pertains to the specific income tax rates and brackets set for that year within Saskatchewan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc0b482656c1fbfddee8368d8616fa29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11a41d513daa1b6c57ef311273744d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dd1ab2e92259b216c4f87314232a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcc35deaf2234a3f8713639ffa324b31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-641c0fe12d176236fe97fad76cedd6ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a71d4ab43b2c554a485b5c3822440d6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772370268</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2023 Tax Year">
      <data key="d0">2023 Tax Year</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2023 Tax Year reflects the applicable tax rates and brackets for calculating federal income tax during that year.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2023 Tax Year includes additional tax policy changes and the implementation of new programs and requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2023 Tax Year signifies the tax rates and brackets that governed income tax in Alberta for that particular year.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2023 Tax Year outlines the income brackets and corresponding tax rates for that year.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2023 Tax Year identifies the applicable tax periods for tax filings in Saskatchewan with defined income brackets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11a41d513daa1b6c57ef311273744d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dd1ab2e92259b216c4f87314232a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcc35deaf2234a3f8713639ffa324b31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a71d4ab43b2c554a485b5c3822440d6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772370274</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2022 Tax Year">
      <data key="d0">2022 Tax Year</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2022 Tax Year details the income tax rates and brackets relevant for taxation processes of that fiscal year.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2022 Tax Year introduces further modifications to Canadian tax policy, including adjustments to various deductions and credits.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2022 Tax Year indicates the tax policies and applicable brackets for income taxation in Alberta during that year.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2022 Tax Year established new top income tax brackets and rates that increased the tax burden on high earners.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2022 Tax Year designates the time frame for income taxation applicable in Saskatchewan, featuring its own tax brackets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11a41d513daa1b6c57ef311273744d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dd1ab2e92259b216c4f87314232a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcc35deaf2234a3f8713639ffa324b31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a71d4ab43b2c554a485b5c3822440d6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772370274</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2021 Tax Year">
      <data key="d0">2021 Tax Year</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2021 Tax Year specifies the income tax rates and applicable brackets for the federal income tax calculation.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2021 Tax Year marks the period during which specific tax policy changes were implemented in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2021 Tax Year refers to the income tax rules and brackets set for taxation in Alberta during that year.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2021 Tax Year reflects the income tax brackets and rates in effect for that period.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2021 Tax Year refers to the period during which the taxable income rates and brackets were defined.&lt;SEP&gt;The 2021 Tax Year represents a specific period during which taxable income brackets and rates were in effect for Saskatchewan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11a41d513daa1b6c57ef311273744d0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dd1ab2e92259b216c4f87314232a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcc35deaf2234a3f8713639ffa324b31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-641c0fe12d176236fe97fad76cedd6ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a71d4ab43b2c554a485b5c3822440d6b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772370268</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Refundable Tax Credits">
      <data key="d0">Non-Refundable Tax Credits</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Refundable Tax Credits are credits that reduce the taxpayer's liability but cannot result in a refund if they exceed the tax owed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101249</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Income Tax Brackets">
      <data key="d0">Income Tax Brackets</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Income Tax Brackets refer to the ranges of taxable income that determine the applicable tax rates for federal income tax in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101249</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Common Non-Refundable Tax Credits">
      <data key="d0">Common Non-Refundable Tax Credits</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Common Non-Refundable Tax Credits are widely applicable tax credits that individuals can claim to reduce their overall tax liability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101249</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Common Deductions from Income">
      <data key="d0">Common Deductions from Income</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Common Deductions from Income represent various allowable deductions that lower taxable income prior to tax calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ca78b2360336d6f6222a6f4c95eb423</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101250</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RRSP Contributions">
      <data key="d0">RRSP Contributions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">RRSP contributions are retirement savings contributions that reduce taxable income up to a deduction limit before tax is calculated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101250</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Union/Professional Dues">
      <data key="d0">Union/Professional Dues</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Union and professional dues are expenses that can be deducted from taxable income, typically reported in Box 44 on T4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101250</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Child Care Expenses">
      <data key="d0">Child Care Expenses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Child care expenses are costs incurred for the care of children that can reduce taxable income.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101250</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Moving Expenses">
      <data key="d0">Moving Expenses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Moving expenses can be deducted if an individual moves 40 or more kilometers closer to work or school.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Employment Expenses">
      <data key="d0">Employment Expenses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Employment expenses are costs required by an employer that can be deducted from taxable income, typically supported by Form T2200.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101251</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Capital Gains Deduction">
      <data key="d0">Capital Gains Deduction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The capital gains deduction applies to qualifying small business shares and farm/fishing property, allowing for tax reductions on capital gains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Northern Residents Deduction">
      <data key="d0">Northern Residents Deduction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Northern residents deduction is a tax benefit that assists residents living in prescribed northern zones, documented using Form T2222.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Federal Tax Calculation">
      <data key="d0">Federal Tax Calculation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Federal tax calculation is a process involving the computation of tax owed based on total income and applicable deductions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Marginal Tax Rate">
      <data key="d0">Marginal Tax Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The marginal tax rate represents the rate imposed on the next dollar of income and influences tax savings derived from deductions.&lt;SEP&gt;The marginal tax rate is the rate at which the interest income is fully taxed without any preferential treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b63e9f386df072fa44d9d07188cfe2b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369478</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Total Income">
      <data key="d0">Total Income</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Total income refers to all earnings received before any deductions are applied, noted on Line 15000 in tax calculations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Net Income">
      <data key="d0">Net Income</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Net income is the amount remaining after deductions are subtracted from total income, as indicated on Line 23600.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101252</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Taxable Income">
      <data key="d0">Taxable Income</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Taxable income is calculated after additional deductions are applied to net income, referenced on Line 26000 in tax documentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Taxable income refers to the amount of income that is subject to tax, which is relevant for calculating the Ontario Health Premium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37cf12753edb4a0b44312876883ae249&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7bba92e1e9b7a1972810e69c85c424</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101289</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Provincial Income Tax">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Provincial Income Tax</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ontario Provincial Income Tax outlines the taxation system for the province of Ontario, Canada, including tax brackets and credits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101284</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Tax Brackets">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Tax Brackets</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ontario Tax Brackets illustrate the progressive taxation system used in Ontario, specifying income ranges and corresponding tax rates for different years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101284</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Taxable Income Range">
      <data key="d0">Taxable Income Range</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Taxable Income Range is a classification of income amounts that determine the tax rates applicable to taxpayers in Ontario, changing each year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101284</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Basic Personal Amount">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Basic Personal Amount</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ontario Basic Personal Amount is a non-refundable tax credit that varies by year and impacts provincial income tax calculations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101285</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Surtax">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Surtax</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ontario Surtax is an additional tax levied on provincial tax that exceeds specified thresholds, affecting high-income earners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101285</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Combined Ontario Marginal Rates">
      <data key="d0">Combined Ontario Marginal Rates</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Combined Ontario Marginal Rates represent the total tax rates applicable to different income ranges combining both federal and provincial rates for the 2024 tax year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101285</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Tax Credits">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Tax Credits</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ontario Tax Credits provide financial relief to residents through various credits based on specific criteria, aiming to reduce tax burdens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101285</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Trillium Benefit">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Trillium Benefit</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ontario Trillium Benefit is a combined tax credit offered in Ontario that includes multiple credits into a single monthly payment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ontario Trillium Benefit is a payment that combines property and energy tax credits for Ontario residents, paid monthly.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ontario Trillium Benefit combines multiple credits to support individuals and families, based on income and residency status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61aebf2ad198d778d2af95f014d9c782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e6767fe1983997e5594e2d1e81f3962</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772370163</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Energy and Property Tax Credit">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Energy and Property Tax Credit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ontario Energy and Property Tax Credit provides financial assistance for property tax or rent expenses, subject to income adjustment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101286</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Northern Ontario Energy Credit">
      <data key="d0">Northern Ontario Energy Credit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Northern Ontario Energy Credit provides financial relief to northern residents, offering amounts up to $180 for singles and $277 for families.&lt;SEP&gt;Northern Ontario Energy Credit is a financial benefit aimed at residents in Northern Ontario, differentiating between singles and families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7bba92e1e9b7a1972810e69c85c424</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101287</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario Sales Tax Credit">
      <data key="d0">Ontario Sales Tax Credit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ontario Sales Tax Credit helps families with tax relief, allowing up to $345 per adult and per child, subject to income adjustments.&lt;SEP&gt;Ontario Sales Tax Credit offers reimbursement for sales tax, adjusted based on family income and number of children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-af3ccb564fd9b7b3591223bd47140183&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7bba92e1e9b7a1972810e69c85c424</data>
