    <node id="Tax Year 2023">
      <data key="d0">Tax Year 2023</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tax Year 2023 refers to the year for which the RRSP maximum new room limit was set at $30,780.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101331</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Overcontribution Penalty">
      <data key="d0">Overcontribution Penalty</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Overcontribution Penalty is a tax applied to contributions exceeding the RRSP deduction limit by more than $2,000, accrues monthly until corrected.&lt;SEP&gt;The overcontribution penalty applies when contributions exceed the allowed TFSA limit, resulting in a 1% tax per month on the excess amount.&lt;SEP&gt;Overcontribution Penalty refers to the penalty tax on contributions exceeding FHSA limits, which is 1% per month on the excess amount.&lt;SEP&gt;The Overcontribution Penalty imposes a 1% per month tax on contributions that exceed the FHSA's annual or lifetime limits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da06f6e3dca271d80baa141c0aef4d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-534ea8e212706659d6a83a6fde877e5d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Form T1-OVP">
      <data key="d0">Form T1-OVP</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">Form T1-OVP is the Individual Tax Return for RRSP, PRPP, and SPP Excess Contributions, which must be filed when overcontributions occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101331</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spousal RRSP">
      <data key="d0">Spousal RRSP</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Spousal RRSP allows an individual to contribute to their spouse's or common-law partner's RRSP, providing tax deductions while the spouse owns the plan.&lt;SEP&gt;Spousal RRSP is a type of RRSP that allows one spouse to contribute to the other's retirement savings to equalize tax burdens during retirement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101362</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Home Buyers' Plan">
      <data key="d0">Home Buyers' Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Home Buyers' Plan (HBP) permits first-time home buyers to withdraw certain funds from their RRSP tax-free to purchase or build a qualifying home.&lt;SEP&gt;The Home Buyers' Plan allows withdrawals from RRSPs for first-time home purchases, with withdrawal limits increased to $60,000 in 2024.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03bb900cab8fd043392cad33c9c60640</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369780</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lifelong Learning Plan">
      <data key="d0">Lifelong Learning Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Lifelong Learning Plan (LLP) allows for RRSP withdrawals for education or training expenses, subject to repayment requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101332</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RRSP Withdrawals">
      <data key="d0">RRSP Withdrawals</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">RRSP Withdrawals are amounts taken from an RRSP, which are generally taxed as income when received.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101332</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RRSP at Death">
      <data key="d0">RRSP at Death</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The RRSP at Death refers to the treatment of an RRSP following the account holder's death, potentially rolling over to a qualifying survivor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101333</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Key Tax Planning Strategies">
      <data key="d0">Key Tax Planning Strategies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Key Tax Planning Strategies with RRSPs include maximizing deductions by contributing in higher tax brackets and utilizing spousal RRSPs for income splitting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101333</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RRSP Contribution Limits">
      <data key="d0">RRSP Contribution Limits</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">RRSP Contribution Limits describe the calculations for the maximum deductible amount for contributions made to a Registered Retirement Savings Plan in a given year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101333</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Annual RRSP Dollar Limits">
      <data key="d0">Annual RRSP Dollar Limits</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Annual RRSP Dollar Limits represent the specific maximum contribution amounts set for each tax year from 2020 to 2025.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1590b8716306682b296c39fb8f8a1c64</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101334</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Charitable Donation Tax Credit">
      <data key="d0">Charitable Donation Tax Credit</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Charitable Donation Tax Credit is a tax incentive based on the fair market value of securities at the time of donation, promoting efficient giving strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;A charitable donation tax credit is available when donating securities in-kind, eliminating capital gains tax and providing a tax credit.&lt;SEP&gt;The Charitable Donation Tax Credit is a non-refundable credit for donations, with higher rates for donations exceeding $200.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8c864baf455d682e0b304e5f7a954e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369351</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Work from Home Expenses">
      <data key="d0">Work from Home Expenses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Work from Home Expenses refer to the costs employees can deduct when required to work from home, including home office expenses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101360</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Employment Deductions">
      <data key="d0">Employment Deductions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Employment Deductions encompass various allowable expenses that employees can claim on their taxes based on their job requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101360</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vehicle Expenses">
      <data key="d0">Vehicle Expenses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Vehicle Expenses are deductions available to employees using personal vehicles for work-related tasks, including fuel and maintenance costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101360</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Expenses">
      <data key="d0">Medical Expenses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical Expenses are costs that can be claimed for tax credits, specifically for expenses exceeding certain thresholds within a 12-month period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101361</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Form T2200">
      <data key="d0">Form T2200</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Form T2200 is a tax form that certifies an employee's eligibility for claiming various work-related expenses, including those for work from home and vehicle usage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101361</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Unused Donation Credits">
      <data key="d0">Unused Donation Credits</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Unused Donation Credits refer to charitable donation tax credits that can be carried forward up to 5 years for tax purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101361</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="29%/33% Federal Credit Rate">
      <data key="d0">29%/33% Federal Credit Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 29%/33% Federal Credit Rate indicates the federal tax credit rates applicable under certain income conditions for charitable donations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101361</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian Tax Planning Strategies">
      <data key="d0">Canadian Tax Planning Strategies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Canadian Tax Planning Strategies refer to various methods and approaches for optimizing tax liabilities in Canada, as outlined in CRA publications and the Income Tax Act.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101362</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TFSA">
      <data key="d0">TFSA</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tax-Free Savings Account (TFSA) is a savings account that allows Canadians to earn tax-free investment income and withdrawals.&lt;SEP&gt;TFSA, or Tax-Free Savings Account, is a savings account in Canada where the contributions and withdrawals do not affect taxable income.&lt;SEP&gt;TFSA stands for Tax-Free Savings Account, providing Canadians the opportunity to earn investment income tax-free.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61aebf2ad198d778d2af95f014d9c782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da06f6e3dca271d80baa141c0aef4d6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Capital Gains">
      <data key="d0">Capital Gains</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Capital gains are the profits made from selling an asset for more than its purchase price, which is subject to tax regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101363</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pension Income Splitting">
      <data key="d0">Pension Income Splitting</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pension income splitting allows individuals receiving qualifying pension income to allocate part of their income to their spouse for tax benefits.&lt;SEP&gt;Pension Income Splitting allows individuals receiving eligible pension income to allocate up to 50% to a spouse for tax purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8c864baf455d682e0b304e5f7a954e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369352</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canada Child Benefit">
      <data key="d0">Canada Child Benefit</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Canada Child Benefit (CCB) is a government program that provides financial support to families with children based on their family income.&lt;SEP&gt;The Canada Child Benefit is a financial support program for families with children, which is also not affected by TFSA withdrawals.&lt;SEP&gt;The Canada Child Benefit is a monthly payment to families based on their previous year's tax return, to assist with child-rearing costs.&lt;SEP&gt;The Canada Child Benefit is a tax-free monthly payment to eligible families with children under 18, based on adjusted family net income.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61aebf2ad198d778d2af95f014d9c782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d66ea5179b3a914a2b6215ce0d8004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369354</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Charitable Donation">
      <data key="d0">Charitable Donation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Charitable donations involve giving to registered charities, which can provide tax credits and other financial benefits to the donor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101364</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RRIF">
      <data key="d0">RRIF</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Registered Retirement Income Fund (RRIF) is a type of account that individuals must withdraw from in retirement and is influenced by previous RRSP contributions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61aebf2ad198d778d2af95f014d9c782</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101364</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="OAS Clawback">
      <data key="d0">OAS Clawback</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Old Age Security (OAS) clawback refers to the reduction of OAS payments based on the recipient's income over a certain threshold.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101364</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Capital Gains Reserve">
      <data key="d0">Capital Gains Reserve</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Capital Gains Reserve allows individuals to claim a capital gain over several years for property sold with deferred payments, subject to specific time limits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-443ee4b9b0cd32b4e6c22127239ed83c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101365</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="securities">
      <data key="d0">securities</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53761e82537ba7b2a5d7a74c8d5799d8</data>
      <data key="d2">The Charitable Donation Tax Credit is determined by the fair market value of the securities at the time of donation.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101366</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tax-Free Savings Account (TFSA)">
      <data key="d0">Tax-Free Savings Account (TFSA)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tax-Free Savings Account (TFSA) is a registered savings account for Canadian residents, allowing tax-free income from investments, with specific rules for contributions and withdrawals.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tax-Free Savings Account (TFSA) is a Canadian account that allows investments to grow tax-free.&lt;SEP&gt;A Tax-Free Savings Account allows for withdrawals that do not count as income for GIS purposes, making it crucial for low-income seniors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8c864baf455d682e0b304e5f7a954e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369349</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CRA">
      <data key="d0">CRA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Canada Revenue Agency (CRA) is the governmental body responsible for tax collection and administration in Canada, providing guidelines for the TFSA.&lt;SEP&gt;The Canada Revenue Agency is responsible for administering tax laws and collecting taxes in Canada.&lt;SEP&gt;CRA, or Canada Revenue Agency, is the Canadian governmental agency responsible for tax collection and administration.&lt;SEP&gt;CRA (Canada Revenue Agency) is the federal agency responsible for administering tax laws in Canada, overseeing federal tax returns from residents including those in Quebec.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61aebf2ad198d778d2af95f014d9c782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc0b482656c1fbfddee8368d8616fa29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-936dddac80917fe456d3727287e9f980</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772370220</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian Residents">
      <data key="d0">Canadian Residents</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Canadian residents are individuals who live in Canada and are eligible to open a Tax-Free Savings Account (TFSA) if they meet certain criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101398</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Social Insurance Number (SIN)">
      <data key="d0">Social Insurance Number (SIN)</data>
      <data key="d1">identifier</data>
      <data key="d2">The Social Insurance Number (SIN) is a unique identifier for individuals in Canada, necessary for opening a TFSA and other official purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;A Social Insurance Number (SIN) is required for eligibility in the First Home Savings Account (FHSA) and is used to track an individual's earnings and contributions in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da06f6e3dca271d80baa141c0aef4d6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369639</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2009–2012 Contribution Limit">
      <data key="d0">2009–2012 Contribution Limit</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The contribution limit for the TFSA from 2009 to 2012 was set at $5,000 per year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101398</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2013–2014 Contribution Limit">
      <data key="d0">2013–2014 Contribution Limit</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The contribution limit for the TFSA for the years 2013 and 2014 was set at $5,500 per year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101398</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2015 Contribution Limit">
      <data key="d0">2015 Contribution Limit</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The contribution limit for the TFSA for the year 2015 was set at $10,000.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101398</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2016–2018 Contribution Limit">
      <data key="d0">2016–2018 Contribution Limit</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The contribution limit for the TFSA from 2016 to 2018 was set at $5,500 per year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101398</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2019–2022 Contribution Limit">
      <data key="d0">2019–2022 Contribution Limit</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The contribution limit for the TFSA from 2019 to 2022 was set at $6,000 per year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101400</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2023 Contribution Limit">
      <data key="d0">2023 Contribution Limit</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The contribution limit for the TFSA for the year 2023 was set at $6,500.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101400</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2024 Contribution Limit">
      <data key="d0">2024 Contribution Limit</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The contribution limit for the TFSA for the year 2024 is set at $7,000.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101400</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="2025 Contribution Limit">
      <data key="d0">2025 Contribution Limit</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">The contribution limit for the TFSA for the year 2025 is also set at $7,000.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101400</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cumulative Contribution Room">
      <data key="d0">Cumulative Contribution Room</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cumulative contribution room represents the total amount a Canadian resident can contribute to their TFSA, which accumulates annually.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101401</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Withdrawal and Recontribution Rules">
      <data key="d0">Withdrawal and Recontribution Rules</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Withdrawal and recontribution rules govern how TFSA withdrawals are treated regarding tax and future contributions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101401</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Qualifying Investments">
      <data key="d0">Qualifying Investments</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Qualifying investments for a TFSA include a wide range of financial instruments that can be held within the account, similar to an RRSP.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101401</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TFSA on Death">
      <data key="d0">TFSA on Death</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The TFSA on death provision determines how the account's value is handled when the holder passes away, impacting tax obligations for designated successors or beneficiaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101402</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TFSA vs RRSP">
      <data key="d0">TFSA vs RRSP</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The TFSA versus RRSP comparison discusses the advantages and considerations for choosing between these two types of registered savings accounts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101402</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CRA Publication RC4466">
      <data key="d0">CRA Publication RC4466</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">CRA Publication RC4466 is the guide for individuals on Tax-Free Savings Accounts (TFSAs), providing essential information about its rules and regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101402</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="OAS">
      <data key="d0">OAS</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Old Age Security (OAS) is a financial assistance program in Canada that provides benefits to seniors, impacting eligibility considerations for TFSAs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101402</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="GIS">
      <data key="d0">GIS</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Guaranteed Income Supplement (GIS) is a financial aid program in Canada designed to support low-income seniors, which can be influenced by TFSA withdrawals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101403</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="GST/HST Credit">
      <data key="d0">GST/HST Credit</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Goods and Services Tax/Harmonized Sales Tax (GST/HST) credit is a payment to help individuals with low or modest incomes; TFSA withdrawals do not affect eligibility for this credit.&lt;SEP&gt;The GST/HST credit is a quarterly benefit payment to assist low and moderate-income individuals with GST or HST expenses.&lt;SEP&gt;The GST/HST Credit is a refundable quarterly tax credit designed to offset the GST/HST paid by low and modest-income individuals and families in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61aebf2ad198d778d2af95f014d9c782&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d66ea5179b3a914a2b6215ce0d8004</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369353</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Old Age Security (OAS)">
      <data key="d0">Old Age Security (OAS)</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8c864baf455d682e0b304e5f7a954e</data>
      <data key="d2">The rules of the Tax-Free Savings Account impact beneficiaries' eligibility for Old Age Security benefits.&lt;SEP&gt;Old Age Security is a government program providing income to seniors, with amounts reduced based on additional income.</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369349</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Guaranteed Income Supplement (GIS)">
      <data key="d0">Guaranteed Income Supplement (GIS)</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8c864baf455d682e0b304e5f7a954e</data>
      <data key="d2">Withdrawals from a Tax-Free Savings Account have implications for eligibility for the Guaranteed Income Supplement.&lt;SEP&gt;The Guaranteed Income Supplement is a supplement to OAS and is reduced by withdrawals from RRSPs, but not from TFSAs.</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369349</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Goods and Services Tax/Harmonized Sales Tax (GST/HST) Credit">
      <data key="d0">Goods and Services Tax/Harmonized Sales Tax (GST/HST) Credit</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4efa231cfe20987fec08d6db7e701fb2</data>
      <data key="d2">TFSA withdrawals do not impact eligibility for the Goods and Services Tax/Harmonized Sales Tax credit.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772101410</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canada">
      <data key="d0">Canada</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Canada is the country mentioned in the context of deemed disposition events such as emigrating which can incur a departure tax.&lt;SEP&gt;Canada is the country where the capital gains taxation rules and exemptions are applied as per the CRA and Income Tax Act.&lt;SEP&gt;Canada is a country where the First Home Savings Account (FHSA) plan is applicable for residents saving for a first-time home purchase.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6da06f6e3dca271d80baa141c0aef4d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11c553d9c05dddfa3309447cb2baa12c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CRA Schedule 3">
      <data key="d0">CRA Schedule 3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">CRA Schedule 3 is a document containing information and regulations regarding the taxation of capital gains in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369119</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Income Tax Act Sections 38-55">
      <data key="d0">Income Tax Act Sections 38-55</data>
      <data key="d1">legal_document</data>
      <data key="d2">Sections 38-55 of the Income Tax Act provide detailed legal guidelines on the treatment of capital gains and losses in Canada.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369119</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Budget 2024">
      <data key="d0">Budget 2024</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Budget 2024 includes updates and provisions affecting capital gains taxation, such as the Canadian Entrepreneurs' Incentive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369119</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Capital Gain">
      <data key="d0">Capital Gain</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A capital gain arises when a capital property is sold for a price higher than its adjusted cost base and associated expenses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369120</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Capital Property">
      <data key="d0">Capital Property</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Capital property includes investments like stocks, bonds, real estate (except the principal residence), mutual funds, and ETFs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369120</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adjusted Cost Base">
      <data key="d0">Adjusted Cost Base</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Adjusted Cost Base is the original purchase price of a capital property, adjusted for distribution, stock splits, and other factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369120</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Principal Residence Exemption">
      <data key="d0">Principal Residence Exemption</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Principal Residence Exemption allows capital gains on the sale of a principal residence to be exempt from tax.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369121</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lifetime Capital Gains Exemption">
      <data key="d0">Lifetime Capital Gains Exemption</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Lifetime Capital Gains Exemption allows a lifetime exemption from tax on capital gains from qualified small business shares and farm/fishing property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369121</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian Entrepreneurs' Incentive">
      <data key="d0">Canadian Entrepreneurs' Incentive</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A legislative incentive providing additional lifetime exemption amounts for Canadian entrepreneurs, starting in 2025.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369121</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tax-Loss Harvesting Strategy">
      <data key="d0">Tax-Loss Harvesting Strategy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A strategy used by investors to offset capital gains by selling investments at a loss and applying the loss against gains.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369121</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Emigrating from Canada">
      <data key="d0">Emigrating from Canada</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Emigrating from Canada triggers deemed disposition of capital property at fair market value, known as departure tax.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369121</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Death">
      <data key="d0">Death</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Death triggers deemed disposition of capital property at fair market value, unless rolled over to a spouse or common-law partner.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369121</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Superficial Loss Rule">
      <data key="d0">Superficial Loss Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A rule that denies a capital loss if a security is sold and repurchased within 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;The Superficial Loss Rule is a tax regulation aimed at preventing tax loss harvesting through the artificial generation of capital losses through repurchasing the same or identical securities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369122</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Capital Gains Inclusion Rate">
      <data key="d0">Capital Gains Inclusion Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Capital Gains Inclusion Rate determines the portion of a capital gain that is taxable, which varies for individuals, corporations, and trusts before and after June 25, 2024.&lt;SEP&gt;The Capital Gains Inclusion Rate determines the percentage of capital gains that are taxable, increased from 50% to 66.67% starting June 25, 2024.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03bb900cab8fd043392cad33c9c60640</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369780</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ontario">
      <data key="d0">Ontario</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ontario is a province in Canada where specific tax rates apply, including a combined marginal rate mentioned in an example calculation of capital gains tax.&lt;SEP&gt;Ontario is a Canadian province that eliminated its provincial tuition tax credit for 2017 and subsequent years.&lt;SEP&gt;Ontario is a Canadian province that indexed its tax brackets and made no major structural changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ontario is one of the Canadian provinces that offers various tax credits and benefits to residents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03bb900cab8fd043392cad33c9c60640&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e6767fe1983997e5594e2d1e81f3962</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772370161</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="66.67% Inclusion Rate">
      <data key="d0">66.67% Inclusion Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The 66.67% Inclusion Rate is applied to capital gains exceeding $250,000 per year for individuals and to all capital gains for corporations and trusts after June 25, 2024.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369122</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Schedule 3">
      <data key="d0">Schedule 3</data>
      <data key="d1">form</data>
      <data key="d2">Schedule 3 is a section of the T1 tax return where capital gains must be reported.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369122</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T5008 Slips">
      <data key="d0">T5008 Slips</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">T5008 slips report securities dispositions and are used for tax purposes, though they may not include ACB, which must be tracked by the investor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369123</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T3 Slips">
      <data key="d0">T3 Slips</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">T3 slips report capital gains from mutual funds and trusts and are used for tax purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;T3 slips report trust income, and are issued to beneficiaries of a trust within the tax year.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61aebf2ad198d778d2af95f014d9c782</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369300</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Principal Residence Exemption (PRE)">
      <data key="d0">Principal Residence Exemption (PRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">taxpolicy</data>
      <data key="d2">The Principal Residence Exemption (PRE) allows real estate dispositions to be exempt from reporting under certain conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369123</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP)">
      <data key="d0">Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) is a Canadian retirement savings account where contributions are tax-deferred.&lt;SEP&gt;A Registered Retirement Savings Plan is a retirement account where withdrawals count as income for GIS purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8c864baf455d682e0b304e5f7a954e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369349</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Departure Tax">
      <data key="d0">Departure Tax</data>
      <data key="d1">taxpolicy</data>
      <data key="d2">The departure tax is a type of deemed disposition tax applied when emigrating from Canada, involving the fair market value assessment of capital property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369124</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Capital Gains Reserves">
      <data key="d0">Capital Gains Reserves</data>
      <data key="d1">taxpolicy</data>
      <data key="d2">Capital gains reserves allow spreading out the gain over a period of up to 5 years, or 10 years for qualifying properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369124</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adjusted Cost Base (ACB)">
      <data key="d0">Adjusted Cost Base (ACB)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjusted Cost Base (ACB) is the concept involving tracking of all purchase prices, reinvested distributions, and return of capital adjustments for tax calculation purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369124</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Family Farm Corporation">
      <data key="d0">Family Farm Corporation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Family Farm Corporation is an organization mentioned in the context of capital gains reserves which have specific tax treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369124</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gifting Property">
      <data key="d0">Gifting Property</data>
      <data key="d1">taxevent</data>
      <data key="d2">Gifting property is considered a deemed disposition where property is transferred as a gift, and fair market value is used for tax assessment to prevent artificial losses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369124</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gains Inclusion Rate">
      <data key="d0">Gains Inclusion Rate</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gains Inclusion Rate refers to the percentage of capital gains that are included in taxable income, typically starting at 50% with additional thresholds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369124</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Deemed Disposition Events">
      <data key="d0">Deemed Disposition Events</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b628c2e9232fe00bd1ee55c3ddc07d</data>
      <data key="d2">Emigrating from Canada is considered a deemed disposition event, requiring tax calculation at fair market value.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369125</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Change in Use">
      <data key="d0">Change in Use</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b52c763122110f77d0bf6ccf9c8f8507</data>
      <data key="d2">In Canada, converting a principal residence to rental property is a deemed disposition event that incurs tax obligations based on fair market value.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369130</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T2202 Slip">
      <data key="d0">T2202 Slip</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The T2202 Slip, also known as the Tuition and Enrolment Certificate, is issued by designated educational institutions in Canada for students to claim tuition amounts on their tax return.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369226</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canadian Universities and Colleges">
      <data key="d0">Canadian Universities and Colleges</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Canadian universities and colleges are designated educational institutions where eligible tuition fees can be paid to qualify for tax credits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369226</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Certified Institutions">
      <data key="d0">Certified Institutions</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Certified institutions offer occupational skills courses with fees eligible for educational tax credits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369226</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Foreign Universities">
      <data key="d0">Foreign Universities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Foreign universities, where students enroll in courses lasting at least 3 consecutive weeks, have fees eligible for Canadian tuition tax credits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369227</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Federal Tuition Tax Credit">
      <data key="d0">Federal Tuition Tax Credit</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Federal Tuition Tax Credit allows for a 15% credit rate of eligible tuition fees from qualifying institutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369227</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tuition Fees">
      <data key="d0">Tuition Fees</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tuition fees are payments made to educational institutions which may qualify for non-refundable tax credits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369227</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mandatory Ancillary Fees">
      <data key="d0">Mandatory Ancillary Fees</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mandatory ancillary fees are included in eligible tuition amounts but exclude student union and health plan fees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369227</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Refundable Tax Credit">
      <data key="d0">Non-Refundable Tax Credit</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-refundable tax credits reduce tax liability but cannot create a refund.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369227</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Schedule 11">
      <data key="d0">Schedule 11</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Schedule 11 of the T1 tax return tracks unused tuition credits and carryforward amounts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369229</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CRA Guide P105">
      <data key="d0">CRA Guide P105</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The CRA Guide P105 is a resource providing information about the T2202 slip and related Canadian tax rules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369230</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Canada Revenue Agency (CRA)">
      <data key="d0">Canada Revenue Agency (CRA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The CRA is the federal agency responsible for tax administration, including overseeing tuition and education credits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-763b1c1e1aaf51a4f6b35169530a488f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772369230</data>
